Before the HTML parser starts, Ladybird looks for a meta charset
by tokenizing the page’s bytes through the first 1024, and on until
the head ends or a template starts, as whatwg/html#6962 proposes.
Blink’s HTMLMetaCharsetParser::CheckForMetaCharset() and WebKit’s
TextResourceDecoder::checkForHeadCharset() tokenize ahead in the same
way, holding back decoding meanwhile, and stop at a tag that can’t be
in the head once at least 1024 bytes have arrived. Gecko implements
the proposal: Its nsHtml5StreamParser keeps looking for a meta charset
(mLookingForMetaCharset) until TemplatePushedOrHeadPopped() past
UNCONDITIONAL_META_SCAN_BOUNDARY, and holds the parse’s tree operations
in a speculation meanwhile, which it drops (DiscardMetaSpeculation())
when it finds one. But Gecko also lets a token that’s open at that
boundary finish (IsInTokenStartedAtKilobyteBoundary()), and Ladybird
doesn’t: Once the head has ended, it stops at byte 1024, even inside
a long comment — so a page whose head ends early doesn’t wait on
the rest of that comment.
